<<<
+ note, n. For synonymy see SIGN. note, v., makes -table; see MUTE E. nothing less than. The OED remarks:— The combination nothing less than has two quite contrary senses ’, & gives as the first * quite equal to, the same thing as’, with, for illustration,
===
**note, n.** For synonymy see ^^SIGN^^. **note, v.,** makes *-table*; see ^^MUTE E^^. **nothing less than.** The OED remarks:—' The combination *nothing less than* has two quite contrary senses', & gives as the first 'quite equal to, the same thing as', with, for illustration,
>>>

<<<
But yet methinks my father’s execution Was nothing less than bloody tyranny; & as the second ‘ far from being, any thing rather than’, with, for illustration, Who, trusting to the laws, expected ».l.t. an attack. To the second sense it adds the description ‘ Now rare’.
===
*But yet methinks my father's execution Was nothing less than bloody tyranny*; & as the second 'far from being, any thing rather than', with, for illustration, *Who, trusting to the laws, expected n. l. t. an attack.* To the second sense it adds the description 'Now rare'.
>>>

<<<
As a matter of grammar, either sense is legitimate, less being different parts of speech in the two, as appears in the light of paraphrases:—my father’s death was no smaller thing than tyranny (i.e., less is an adjective);
===
As a matter of grammar, either sense is legitimate, *less* being different parts of speech in the two, as appears in the light of paraphrases:—my father's death was *no smaller thing than* tyranny (i. e., *less* is an adjective);
>>>

<<<
they expected nothing in a lower degree than they expected an attack (i.e., less is an adverb); grammar, then, leaves the matter open.
===
they expected *nothing in a lower degree than they expected* an attack (i. e., *less* is an adverb); grammar, then, leaves the matter open.
>>>

<<<
If the sense of they expected n.l.t. an attack did not happen to be fixed by trusting to the laws, who would dare decide whether they expected it very much or very little?
===
If the sense of *they expected n. l. t. an attack* did not happen to be fixed by *trusting to the laws,* who would dare decide whether they expected it very much or very little?
>>>

<<<
puzzling:—It recognizes also both the necessity of reform & liberation from dead dogmas & rubrics, & the impossibility of reform coming from a House of Commons desiring nothing less than to occupy its debates with discussions of the validity of the thirty-nine articles.
===
puzzling:—*It recognizes also both the necessity of reform & liberation from dead dogmas & rubrics, & the impossibility of reform coming from a House of Commons desiring* nothing less than *to occupy its debates with discussions of the validity of the thirty-nine articles.*
>>>

<<<
 / Now we are introduced to inspired ‘ crowd-men’ or heroes who have a passion for making order out of the human chaos & finding expression for the real soul of the people; these heroes or crowdmen resemble n.1.t. the demagogue as popularly conceived,
===
/*Now we are introduced to inspired 'crowd-men' or heroes who have a passion for making order out of the human chaos & finding expression for the real soul of the people; these heroes or crowd-men* resemble n. l. t. *the demagogue as popularly conceived.*
>>>

<<<
+ notice makes -ceable; see MUTE E. notify. For inflexions see VERBS IN -IE, -Y, -YE, 6. nought. See NAUGHT. noumenon. Pronounce now’min6n; pl. -ena, see -ON 1.
===
**notice** makes *-ceable*; see ^^MUTE E^^. **notify.** For inflexions see ^^VERBS IN -IE, -Y, -YE^^, 6. **nought.** See ^^NAUGHT^^. **noumenon.** Pronounce now'mĭnŏn; pl. *-ena*, see ^^-ON^^ 1.
>>>

<<<
noun has two adjectives—nominal & nounal, but is comfortable with neither.
===
**noun** has two adjectives—*nominal* & *nounal*, but is comfortable with neither.
>>>

<<<
This has induced grammarians to form the word from which they of all people should have shrunk—nounal. It is what is described in the article HYBRID DERIVATIVES as a spurious hybrid; see that article for a discussion of coastal & similar words.
===
This has induced grammarians to form the word from which they of all people should have shrunk—*nounal*. It is what is described in the article ^^HYBRID DERIVATIVES^^ as a *spurious hybrid*; see that article for a discussion of *coastal* & similar words.
>>>

<<<
The grammarian’s right course is to work with the word noun as far as possible, & when an adjective or adverb is indispensable, use nominal(ly).
===
The grammarian's right course is to work with the word *noun* as far as possible, &, when an adjective or adverb is indispensable, use *nominal(ly)*.
>>>

